{"id": 13, "ts": 1779494379.0540602, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 1, "agent": "agent_2", "message": "I\u2019d like to add a few observations from my interview notes that have not yet been shared.\n- **Candidate\u202fA**: while he demonstrates strong situational awareness, complex\u2011systems insight and leadership, he is perceived by colleagues as a show\u2011off and is not very open to new ideas. This raises concerns for crew cooperation, professional communication and adaptability.\n- **Candidate\u202fB**: he is very conscientious, handles stress well and has solid weather\u2011assessment and computer skills, but I noted a relatively weak memory for numbers and that he makes nasty remarks about colleagues. These issues could affect information accuracy and crew\u2011team dynamics.\n- **Candidate\u202fD**: he concentrates well, solves problems exceptionally and takes responsibility seriously, yet is seen as a know\u2011it\u2011all and has a hot temper, which may impair cooperation and stress resilience in long\u2011haul operations.\nCompared with these, **Candidate\u202fC** still shows the most comprehensive fit: 100\u202f% reliability, quick correct decisions, solid technical competence, calmness in crises and a positive crew atmosphere. The remaining gaps are his communication style, egocentric attitude and lack of demonstrated feedback handling.\nGiven the current evidence, I provisionally support **Candidate\u202fC** as the leading alternative, while we should seek additional concrete examples of how C manages feedback and whether his communication style could be mitigated through training. We might also probe the impact of B\u2019s numeric memory issues and D\u2019s temper under high\u2011stress, long\u2011duration scenarios to ensure no hidden safety concerns.\nMETADATA_JSON:\n{\"agent\": \"agent_2\", \"vote\": \"Candidate C\"}"}
